                    coach_r1_overconfident: `<strong>A target that high misanchors your price band.</strong> At full premium your baseline win probability is ~35%; assuming you’ll win most of the time invites you to over-price and under-justify. Set a realistic target and earn the rest with evidence.`,
                    coach_r1_defeatist:     `<strong>A target that low becomes a self-fulfilling discount.</strong> If you assume you’ll lose, you’ll cut price to compensate and breach the floor. Win probability is something you build with differentiation, not something you concede up front.`,
                    coach_r2_calibrated:    `<strong>A calibrated scope is the disciplined play.</strong> Differentiated modules, coaching and a capstone mapped to the strategy reset, priced to the cap — rich enough to justify the premium, lean enough to clear the margin floor.`,
                    coach_r2_premium:       `<strong>The richest possible design is magnificent and unaffordable.</strong> Maximal scope drives the cost base toward the cap and your contribution margin toward zero — a perfect proposal that loses on price or loses money. What do you trade to hold the floor?`,
                    coach_r2_lean:          `<strong>A stripped, standardized core surrenders your differentiator.</strong> Undercut on price with a lean design and you are now competing as a cheaper version of the consultancy — on its terms, and without its cost advantage. The impact promise collapses.`,
                    coach_r2_defensive:     `<strong>A defensive discount tells the market the premium is negotiable.</strong> You may lift win probability, but you devalue every other bid Meridian makes this year and erode the margin. Hold a calibrated premium and defend it with value.`,
                    coach_r3_blended:       `<strong>A blended faculty model holds the quality bar affordably.</strong> Flagship anchors lend credibility, regional fellows and vetted adjuncts give reach and capacity, and co-teaching protects the standard — all without bankrupting the engagement on airfare.`,
                    coach_r3_flagship:      `<strong>Flying your top faculty to all three regions blows capacity and cost.</strong> There are only ~18 of them, they are overcommitted, and at ~$9,500 a trip the delivery budget evaporates. Either the program can’t be staffed or your margin is gone. What is the blended model?`,
                    coach_r3_adjunct:       `<strong>Local adjuncts alone undercut the quality bar.</strong> Cheapest staffing protects cost but hollows out the very faculty credibility that justifies your premium and underwrites the impact promise. The committee will see a discount school, not Meridian.`,
                    coach_r3_dashboard:     `<strong>A slick dashboard is not an impact methodology.</strong> The consulting firm sells analytics as its core strength; buying tooling without a real baseline-to-end-line measure means you’ve been out-measured on the one mandatory requirement. What exactly do you baseline and measure at end-line?`,
                    coach_r3_satisfaction:  `<strong>Satisfaction surveys are the “happy sheets” the RFP explicitly rejects.</strong> Modern buyers demand outcome evidence, not feedback forms. On the deciding requirement you’ve ceded the battleground to a measurement-led rival.`,
                    coach_r3_strong:        `<strong>This is a credible, defensible faculty-and-impact plan.</strong> A blended bench holds the quality bar within capacity, and a baseline-to-end-line methodology tied to the bank’s business results gives the committee the measured difference your premium needs.`,
                    coach_r3_overtravel:    `<strong>Good faculty model, but you’re over-flying it.</strong> Beyond a couple of dozen international trips the marginal faculty gain is small and the airfare quietly eats your margin. Anchor the regions with flagship presence, then lean on fellows and co-teaching.`,
                    coach_r3_undertravel:   `<strong>Too little flagship presence and the global quality bar collapses.</strong> A two-year, three-region program with almost no international anchoring reads as a local-adjunct program with a Meridian badge. Commit enough flagship trips to underwrite the premium.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That is an answer a procurement committee can write into a recommendation.</strong> Measurable outcomes, global reach, faculty quality and accountability — the value differential evidenced, not asserted. You’ve justified the gap to the cheaper rival without touching the floor.`,
                    coach_r4_assert:        `<strong>“We’re simply better” is not an answer.</strong> A committee cannot write reputation into a recommendation, and the consultancy’s dashboard looks more concrete on the page. Give them the measurable difference — or you’ve lost on price after all.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>You cut to undercut the consultancy and breached the floor.</strong> Win probability looks great, but your contribution margin is below 25%, your finance director won’t approve it, and you’ve told the market Meridian’s premium is negotiable. Was buying this engagement at a loss right, or should you have walked?`,
                    coach_r4_nowalkaway:    `<strong>A walk-away that low is no discipline at all.</strong> If you’ll win the work below your margin floor, you don’t have a walk-away threshold — you have a blank cheque. Know which engagements are not worth winning, even with the renewal pipeline behind them.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Impact-led win theme with a mandatory-requirement edge.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Win theme not anchored on measured impact.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Calibrated scope and a defensible premium price.`,
                    sum_r2_lean:            `Discounted or stripped — differentiation surrendered.`,
                    sum_r2_rich:            `Maximal scope — margin and price exposed.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Blended bench plus a baseline-to-end-line methodology.`,
                    sum_r3_flagship:        `Flagship-only staffing — capacity and travel cost exposed.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Faculty or impact plan undercuts the premium.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Premium evidenced, walk-away discipline held.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Premium asserted or floor breached.`,

                    insight_won:            `Bid won, premium defended: an impact-led, peer-credible proposal that clears the 25% margin floor and is staffable within the bench. You proved the premium buys measurably better outcomes — not just a higher invoice — and out-measured the cheaper rival on the one mandatory requirement.`,
                    insight_floor:          `You breached the 25% contribution-margin floor — your finance director won’t approve this bid, or you’ve bought the engagement at a loss. Winning below the floor devalues every other bid Meridian makes this year and forfeits the discipline behind the premium.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short of a winning, defensible bid. Most likely you under-differentiated on impact, mis-anchored the price against the rival’s ~$3.4M, or ceded the mandatory impact requirement. A premium provider that is merely competitive loses to “good enough.”`,
                    insight_lowimpact:      `Client-impact score stayed low: a vague or token methodology on the one mandatory requirement. A procurement committee will find the consultancy’s measurement story more convincing — and that is increasingly the deciding factor in custom-program awards.`,
                    insight_lowfaculty:     `Faculty fit is weak — the staffing model undercut the quality bar that justifies your premium and underwrites the impact promise. Either the bench was over-flown into cost, or it was hollowed out with adjuncts.`,
                    insight_atcap:          `You bid at the full $4.2M cap. A bid at the ceiling needs an airtight value story; against a 20%-cheaper rival, every dollar of the premium must be evidenced or the committee defaults to price.`,
                    insight_defensible:     `This is a defensible premium: win probability, measured impact and faculty quality moving together above a held margin floor. The committee can write down exactly why Meridian is worth the gap.`,

                    hint_floor:             `Your margin is below the 25% floor — finance won’t approve it. Lift the bid price toward the cap or trim the cost base (scope or over-flown faculty); a won bid that loses money is a loss.`,
                    hint_impact:            `Client-impact score is weak — you are losing the one mandatory requirement to a measurement-led rival. Commit to a baseline-to-end-line methodology tied to the bank’s business results.`,
                    hint_faculty:           `Faculty fit is dangerously low. A blended bench — flagship anchors plus regional fellows and co-teaching — holds the quality bar within capacity without blowing the travel budget.`,
                    hint_behind:            `Your win probability is low with little time left. Re-anchor the price against the rival’s ~$3.4M and evidence the premium with measured outcomes, not assertions.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — margin held above the floor with healthy impact and win probability. Now defend the premium to the committee with the measured value differential and a disciplined walk-away.`,

